{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310675,"nonce":"SHlfAdNDLe9jFOKLnh2WPSOfxzc=","title":"FIXED 98110dc3. Sys.println wrote payload and newline as TWO locked writes, so concurrent lines MERGED and read as ~0.5% loss. ...","message":"**FIXED 2026-08-20, 98110dc3.** Kept because the diagnosis is the reusable part.\n\n\n`haxe_string_println` did `haxe_string_print(s)` then\n`write_stdout_buffered(b\"\\n\")` — TWO acquisitions of the buffer mutex. A second\nthread printing in the gap landed between a line and its newline:\n\n```\n[main] 6[worker] 10\n[worker] 37[main] 31\n```\n\n158 merged lines accounted for all 160 \"missing\" ones. Counting by line makes a\nmerge look like a loss. FIX: one `write_stdout_parts(head, tail, force)` under a\nsingle lock.\n\nSECOND, WORSE PATH: `trace()`, `haxe_sys_print_int/float/bool` and\n`haxe_sys_println()` used `print!`/`println!` STRAIGHT TO THE FD, bypassing the\nbuffer — racing whatever was queued rather than merely interleaving. Now routed\nthrough the same writer. STILL EXPOSED: `haxe_trace_enum_boxed`\n(type_system.rs) builds a line from ~24 direct `print!` calls; reached only by\n`trace()` on an enum.\n\n\n```haxe\nstatic function noisy():Void { var i=0; while (i\u003c5000) { Sys.println(\"[worker] \"+i); i++; } }\n// main spawns two noisy threads, then prints 10000 lines itself\nrun1 main=9945  worker=9902   (expect 10000 / 10000)\nrun2 main=9954  worker=9912\nrun3 main=9968  worker=9929\n```\n\n~0.5% lost, BOTH from the main thread and from the workers. No error, no\nwarning, process exits 0 and the final line arrives.\n\n\n- **NOT volume.** 21,598 lines from ONE thread: 21598/21598, three times.\n- **NOT thread presence.** Main printing 21,598 lines while two threads SPIN\n  (no printing): 21598/21598, three times.\n\n**The loss needs two threads PRINTING at once.** Points at a non-atomic shared\nbuffer or an unsynchronised write in the println path — not at stdout itself.\n\n\nChasing a 3-line discrepancy in nue's `NUE_DUMP_BLOCK_SHAPES=trace` output\nbetween HEAD and a change that provably could not alter routing.\n\n**THE TELL, and it is reusable: `enter` and `before_attn_residual` are emitted\nunconditionally in the SAME straight-line body, so their counts MUST be equal.\nHEAD had 88/88; the other tree had 89/86.** An impossible inequality is lost\noutput, not a behaviour change — you can conclude that WITHOUT knowing the\nmechanism. Look for two counters that must be equal before spending hours on a\ndiff.\n\nAlso: **the nue trace stream reorders between two runs of the SAME tree** — it\nis not a valid oracle at all, and STAGE1_PLAN.md step 6's \"confirm the emitted\ntrace lines match a HEAD run\" cannot be satisfied by construction. Arm A\n(`bench/plan/extract.sh`) IS an oracle: byte-identical across two HEAD runs on\nboth models.\n\n\n**`2\u003e\u00261` IS NOT A CAPTURE.** Folding stderr into stdout interleaves two\nindependent fds; no runtime fix makes that deterministic. nue's block-shape\ntrace varied run-to-run under `2\u003e\u00261` and was STABLE at 21598 three times with\n`2\u003e/dev/null`. Capture the streams separately before calling any output\nnon-deterministic.\n\n**A near-deterministic stream is still not an oracle.** Even after the fix and\nwith stderr separated, the 21,598-line trace moves by 1-3 lines under heavy box\nload; the direction follows whichever tree is more contended, not which code is\nnewer. `bench/plan/extract.sh` arm A IS an oracle (byte-identical across two\nHEAD runs on both models). Use it; do not promote a debug trace to one.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_println_lossy_under_concurrency` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}